  "You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nA restaurant is having a contest to see which customer eats the most food.  The first customer ate 500 grams of food.  The second customer ate 235 grams less than the first customer.  The third customer ate 200 grams more than the first customer.  How many grams of food did the third customer eat?\n<reasoning>\nWe know that the first customer ate 500 grams of food.  The second customer ate 235 grams less than the first customer.  The third customer ate 200 grams more than the first customer.  So, we can use the equation 500 + 235 - 200 to find the third customer's weight.  This gives us 635 grams.  So, the third customer ate 635 grams of food.\n</reasoning>\n<answer>635</answer>\n</problem>\n<problem>\nYou are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nYour friend has an old phone that does not work.  You tell him to go and buy a new phone.  You bought a new phone for your friend.  You pay $200 for the phone.  The phone company also charges you $25 for delivery.  You pay $25 to the delivery person.  How much did you pay for the phone?\n<reasoning>\nWe know that the phone company charges you $25 for delivery.  We also know that the delivery person charges you $25 for delivery.  So, we can use the equation $25 + $25 to find the total amount paid for the phone.  This gives us $50.  So, you paid $50 for the phone.\n</reasoning>\n<answer>50</answer>\n</problem>\n<problem>\nYou are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </