  'You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nConsider a sequence of squares $S_1, S_2, S_3, \\dots$, where each square $S_i$ has side length $a_i$ and area $A_i = a_i^2$. Let $P_n$ be the perimeter of the rectangle formed by arranging the first $n$ squares in a row, and let $Q_n$ be the sum of the perimeters of all possible rectangles formed by choosing two squares from the first $n$ squares. If you are given the value of $P_{100}$, can you uniquely determine the value of $Q_n$ for some integer $n$?\n</reasoning>\n<answer>1840</answer>\n</problem>\n<problem id="problem-168">\n<problemheader>Problem 168</problemheader>\n<problemmath>\n<math>\n<mrow>\n<mi>P</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mo>⋯</mo>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>20</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math>\n<problemmath>\n<math>\n<mrow>\n<mi>P</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mo>⋯</mo>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>20</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math>\n<problemmath>\n<math>\n<mrow>\n<mi>P</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mo>⋯</mo>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n<mi>a</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mfrac>\n<mrow>\n