  'You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nAn object is thrown upward at $60$ feet per second from a 40 feet high platform. Its height in feet at time $t$ seconds is given by $h(t) = 40t - 32t^2$. Find the total time in seconds for the object to fall to the ground.\n\n<reasoning>\nThe time the object is in the air is the time it takes to reach the maximum height. In other words, when the object is at its maximum height, the velocity is zero. The velocity function is found by taking the derivative of $h(t)$, which is the acceleration function. Setting the derivative to zero gives the time when the object is at its maximum height.\n</reasoning>\n\n<answer>\n$8.0$\n</answer>\n</code><eos>'
