  '<problem>\n<statement>\n<p>Consider the function $f(x) = 5x - 2$. If $f(x)$ is a linear function and $f(3x + 2) = 22$, what is $f(-1)$?</p>\n</statement>\n<solution>\n<p>$f(x) = 5x - 2$ is a linear function. Therefore, it can be written as $y = mx + b$, where $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept. Since $f(3x + 2) = 22$, we can substitute $3x + 2$ for $x$ in the equation to get $f(3x + 2) = 5(3x + 2) - 2 = 15x + 10 - 2 = 15x + 8$. Equating this to $22$, we get $15x + 8 = 22$, which simplifies to $15x = 14$, and finally $x = 14/15$. Now we can find $f(-1)$ by substituting $x = -1$ into $f(x) = 5x - 2$ to get $f(-1) = 5(-1) - 2 = -7$.</p>\n</solution>\n</problem>\n<answer>\n<p>$f(-1) = -7$</p>\n</answer>\n</solution>\n</problem>\n<problem>\n<statement>\n<p>The function $f(x) = 5x - 2$ is a linear function. If $f(x) = 22$, then what is $f(-1)$?</p>\n</statement>\n<solution>\n<p>Since $f(x) = 5x - 2$ is a linear function, it can be written in the form $y = mx + b$, where $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept. Since $f(x) = 22$, we can substitute $x = -1$ into the equation to get $f(-1) = 5(-1) - 2 = -7$.</p>\n</solution>\n</problem>\n<problem>\n<statement>\n<p>The function $f(x) = 5x - 2$ is a linear function. If $f(x) = 22$, then what is $f(-1)$?</p>\n</statement>\n<solution>\n<p>Since $f(x) = 5x - 2$ is a linear function, it can be written in the form $y = mx + b$, where $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept. Since $f(x) = 22$, we can substitute $x = -1$ into the equation to get $f(-1) = 5(-1) - 2 = -7$.</p>\n</solution>\n</problem>\n<problem>\n<statement>\n<p>The function $f(x) = 5x - 2$ is a linear function. If $f(x) = 22$, then what is $f(-1)$?</p>\n</statement>\n<solution>\n<p>Since $f(x) = 5x - 2$ is a linear function, it can be written in the form $y = mx + b$, where $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept. Since $f(x) = 22$, we can substitute $x = -1$ into the equation to get $f(-1) = 5(-1) - 2 = -7$.</p>\n</solution>\n</problem