  'Given the polynomial $P(x) = x^{3} + 2x^{2} + x + 1$, find $P(2)$ and $P(-2)$.\n\n[Answer]\n\n<answer>\n<text>\n<inline-math>$P(2) = 27, P(-2) = -33$</inline-math>\n</text>\n</answer>\n<reasoning>\n<text>\nThe polynomial $P(x) = x^{3} + 2x^{2} + x + 1$ can be factored into $P(x) = (x - 2)(x^{2} + x + 1)$. So, $P(2) = (2 - 2)(2^{2} + 2 + 1) = 0(4 + 2 + 1) = 0 \\cdot 7 = 0$ and $P(-2) = (-2 - 2)(-2^{2} + (-2) + 1) = (-4 + 2 + 1)(-4 + 2 + 1) = 0(0) = 0$\n</text>\n</reasoning>\n<example>\n<text>\nAn example is to find $P(2)$.\n</text>\n</example>\n<image>\n<image-link>\n<image-source>https://media.cheggcdn.com/study/d0c/d0c50c28-3738-4157-80e3-15c6f8a0f498/2494-2014-02-12-17-18-38-1.png</image-source>\n</image-link>\n<image-caption>\n<text>$P(x) = (x - 2)(x^{2} + x + 1)$</text>\n</image-caption>\n</image>\n<image>\n<image-link>\n<image-source>https://media.cheggcdn.com/study/679/67922548-1474-4b88-8f4f-173450e04974/2494-2014-02-12-17-18-38-2.png</image-source>\n</image-link>\n<image-caption>\n<text>$P(2) = (2 - 2)(2^{2} + 2 + 1) = 0(4 + 2 + 1) = 0 \\cdot 7 = 0$</text>\n</image-caption>\n</image>\n<image>\n<image-link>\n<image-source>https://media.cheggcdn.com/study/4a1/4a17ec16-04c6-4032-a893-934f8c65f55b/2494-2014-02-12-17-18-38-3.png</image-source>\n</image-link>\n<image-caption>\n<text>$P(-2) = (-2 - 2)(-2^{2} + (-2) + 1) = (-4 + 2 + 1)(-4 + 2 + 1) = 0(0) = 0$</text>\n</image-caption>\n</image>\n<example>\n<text>\nAn example is to find $P(-2)$.\n</text>\n</example>\n</model>\n<eos>'
