  'You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nIn a certain country, there are $n$ cities, and each city is either a "source" or a "sink". A source city has a power plant that produces energy, while a sink city has a factory that consumes energy. The government wants to build a network of energy transmission lines between cities such that:\n\n* Each source city is connected to exactly two sink cities.\n* Each sink city is connected to at most two source cities.\n* No two cities are connected by more than one transmission line.\n* The total number of transmission lines is minimized.\n\nLet $\\mathcal{T}_{n}$ be the set of all possible transmission line configurations for $n$ cities. Find the number of elements in $\\mathcal{T}_{8}$, assuming that there are 3 source cities and 5 sink cities.\n<reasoning>\nTo minimize the number of transmission lines, we want to connect each source city to two sink cities, each sink city to at most two source cities, and no two cities to be connected by more than one transmission line. This means we need to find a way to partition the cities into two disjoint sets: one set of source cities and one set of sink cities. We can then connect each source city to one sink city, and each sink city to the other sink city in the same set.\n\nTo find the number of elements in $\\mathcal{T}_{8}$, we can use the principle of inclusion-exclusion. Let $N$ be the set of all possible transmission line configurations, and let $S$ be the set of all source cities and $D$ be the set of all sink cities. Then, we have:\n\n$$N = |S| \\cdot |D| - |S \\cap D|$$\n\nWhere $|S|$ is the number of elements in $S$, $|D|$ is the number of elements in $D$, and $|S \\cap D|$ is the number of elements in $S \\cap D$.\n\nSince we want to find the number of elements in $\\mathcal{T}_{8}$, we have 