  'You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nGiven that $a^2-b^2=12$ and $a^4+b^4=82$, find $a^6-b^6$.\n\n<reasoning>\nBy the quadratic formula, $a^2-b^2 = (a-b)(a+b)$. Substituting this into the problem, we get $(a-b)(a+b) = 12$. Taking the square root, we get $a-b = \\pm \\sqrt{12}$. Therefore, $a-b = \\sqrt{12}$ or $a-b = -\\sqrt{12}$.\n\nNow, $a^4 + b^4 = (a^2 + b^2)^2 - 2a^2b^2$. Substituting $a^2-b^2 = 12$, we get $(a^2 + b^2)^2 - 2a^2b^2 = 12$. Taking the square root, we get $a^2 + b^2 = \\pm \\sqrt{12}$. Therefore, $a^2 + b^2 = \\sqrt{12}$ or $a^2 + b^2 = -\\sqrt{12}$.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, we get $c^2 = \\sqrt{12}$.\n\nTherefore, $a^6-b^6 = (a^3+b^3)(a^3-b^3)$.\n\nUsing the identity $(a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2) = a^3+b^3$, we get $(a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2) = a^3+b^3$.\n\nUsing the identity $(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2) = a^3-b^3$, we get $(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2) = a^3-b^3$.\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get $a^3-b^3 = (a-b)(a+b)$. Substituting $a-b = \\sqrt{12}$, we get $(a-b)(a+b) = 12$.\n\nTherefore, $a^6-b^6 = \\frac{(a-b)(a+b)(a+b)}{(a+b)(a+b)(a+b)}$.\n</reasoning>\n\n<answer>\n$a^6-b^6 = \\frac{12}{12}$.\n</answer>\n</problem>\n<unused54>, find the area of the triangle.</answer></answer>\n</problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<problem>\n<