  'You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\n<reasoning>Let $a=b+c$. Then $a^2-b^2=c^2$ and $a^4+b^4=(a^2)^2+2ab^2+b^4$.</reasoning>\n\n<answer>Let $a=14$. Then $a^2-b^2=c^2=23^2=529$. Therefore, $a^4+b^4=a^2(a^2-b^2)+b^4=14(529)+23^4=8216$.</answer>\n<unused56>problems/s/s12.tex\n<unused53><unused55>2$ for every $p,q$. Since the GCDs are $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $1$, the LCM is 2. Therefore, the GCD is 1, and the LCM is 2.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\end{problem}\n<unused54>\\begin{problem}[GCD and LCM]\nGiven that $gcd(p,q)=1$ and $lcm(p,q)=2$, find $gcd(4p,4q)$.\n\\begin{enumerate}[label={\\alph*)}]\n\\item $1$\n\\item $2$\n\\item $3$\n\\item $4$\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\begin{solution}\nLet $gcd(p,q)=1$ and $lcm(p,q)=2$. Since $gcd(p,q)=1$, $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime. Therefore, the GCD of $4p$ and $4q$ is the GCD of $4$ and $4$, which is 4. The LCM is <unused56>problems/s/s10.tex\n\\begin{problem}[LCM]\nGiven that $gcd(p,q)=2$ and $lcm(p,q)=10$, find $gcd(p,q)$.\n\\begin{enumerate}[label={\\alph*)}]\n\\item $1$\n\\item $2$\n\\item $3$\n\\item $4$\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\begin{solution}\nLet $gcd(p,q)=2$ and $lcm(p,q)=10$. Since $gcd(p,q)=2$, $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime. Therefore, the GCD of $p$ and $q$ is $1$, and the LCM of $p$ and $q$ is $5$. The LCM of $p$ and $q$ is $10$. Therefore, $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime, and the LCM is 5. Since the LCM is 5, the GCD is 1.\n\\end{solution}\n\\end{problem}\n<unused56>problems/s/s11.tex\n<unused55>\n\\end{problem}\n<unused53>\\begin{problem}[GCD]\nGiven that $gcd(p,q)=1$ and $lcm(p,q)=10$, find $gcd(p,q)$.\n\\begin{enumerate}[label={\\alph*)}]\n\\item $1$\n\\item $2$\n\\item $3$\n\\item $4$\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\begin{solution}\nLet $gcd(p,q)=1$ and $lcm(p,q)=10$. Since $gcd(p,q)=1$, $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime. Therefore, the GCD of $p$ and $q$ is $1$, and the LCM of $p$ and $q$ is $10$. The LCM of $p$ and $q$ is $10$. Therefore, $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime, and the LCM is 10. Since the GCD is 1, the LCM is 10.\n\\end{solution}\n\\end{problem}\n<unused54>\\begin{problem}[GCD and LCM]\nGiven that $gcd(p,q)=1$ and $lcm(p,q)=2$, find $gcd(4p,4q)$.\n\\begin{enumerate}[label={\\alph*)}]\n\\item $1$\n\\item $2$\n\\item $3$\n\\item $4$\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\begin{solution}\nLet $gcd(p,q)=1$ and $lcm(p,q)=2$. Since $gcd(p,q)=1$, $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime. Therefore, the GCD of $4p$ and $4q$ is the GCD of $4$ and $4$, which is 4. The LCM is 2. Therefore, the GCD is 1, and the LCM is 2.<unused56>problems/s/s1.tex\n<unused53><unused55>\n\\end{problem}\n<unused54>\\begin{problem}[GCD]\nGiven that $gcd(p,q)=2$ and $lcm(p,q)=40$, find $gcd(p,q)$.\n\\begin{enumerate}[label={\\alph*)}]\n\\item $1$\n\\item $2$\n\\item $3$\n\\item $4$\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\begin{solution}\nLet $gcd(p,q)=2$ and $lcm(p,q)=40$. Since $gcd(p,q)=2$, $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime. Therefore, the GCD of $p$ and $q$ is $1$, and the LCM of $p$ and $q$ is $20$. The LCM of $p$ and $q$ is $40$. Therefore, $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime, and the LCM is 20. Since the GCD is 1, the LCM is 40.\n\\end{solution}\n\\end{problem}\n\\begin{problem}[GCD and LCM]\nGiven that $gcd(p,q)=1$ and $lcm(p,q)=2$, find $gcd(4p,4q)$.\n\\begin{enumerate}[label={\\alph*)}]\n\\item $1$\n\\item $2$\n\\item $3$\n\\item $4$\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\begin{solution}\nLet $gcd(p,q)=1$ and $lcm(p,q)=2$. Since $gcd(p,q)=1$, $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime. Therefore, the GCD of $4p$ and $4q$ is the GCD of $4$ and $4$, which is 4. The LCM is 2. Therefore, the GCD is 1, and the LCM is 2.<unused56>problems/s/s16.tex\n<unused53>\\begin{problem}[GCD and LCM]\nGiven that $gcd(p,q)=1$ and $lcm(p,q)=2$, find $gcd(4p,4q)$.\n\\begin{enumerate}[label={\\alph*)}]\n\\item $1$\n\\item $2$\n\\item $3$\n\\item $4$\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\begin{solution}\nLet $gcd(p,q)=1$ and $lcm(p,q)=