  "You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nA sphere is inscribed in a cone with a base radius of 6 cm and a height of 20 cm. The volume of the sphere is 288π cm^3. What is the ratio of the radius of the sphere to the radius of the cone's base? Express your answer as a common fraction.\n\n<reasoning>We know that the volume of a cone is given by <tex>$\\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2h$</tex>, where <tex>$r$</tex> is the radius and <tex>$h$</tex> is the height. In this case, we have <tex>$\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (6)^2(20)$</tex> cm^3 for the volume of the cone. Since the volume of the sphere is 288π cm^3, we can set up the equation <tex>$\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (6)^2(20) = 288π$</tex> and solve for <tex>$r$</tex>. <tex>$20(6)^2 = 288$</tex> <tex>$360 = 288$</tex> <tex>$r = 1$</tex> cm\n\nWe know that the radius of the sphere is equal to the radius of the cone's base. Therefore, the ratio of the radius of the sphere to the radius of the cone's base is 1:1.</reasoning>\n\n<answer>1:1</answer>\n<unused56>content/lessons/math/11-3-2_0.0_0_0.1.md\n<unused53><unused55><unused54>\nThis model is from [https://www.gutenberg.org/files/48561/48561-h/48561-h.htm#d1e170](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/48561/48561-h/48561-h.htm#d1e170).\nYou are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\n<reasoning>The ratio of the distance traveled to the time of travel is known as speed. In this case, we are given that the distance traveled is 12 miles and the time of travel is 30 minutes. We can convert 30 minutes to hours by dividing by 60, so the time of travel is 0.5 hours. The speed is then <tex>$\\frac{12}{0.5} = 24$</tex> miles per hour.</reasoning>\n\n<answer>24</answer>\n<unused56>content/lessons/math/11-3-6_0.0_0_0.1.md\n<unused55><unused53>\nThis model is from [https://www.gutenberg.org/files/48561/48561-h/48561-h.htm#d1e233](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/48561/48561-h/48561-h.htm#d1e233).\nYou are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\n<reasoning>The area of a circle is given by the formula <tex>$A = \\pi r^2$</tex>, where <tex>$r$</tex> is the radius of the circle. In this case, we are given the circumference of the circle, which is 60 cm. The formula for the circumference of a circle is <tex>$C = 2\\pi r$</tex>. We can use the equation <tex>$2\\pi r = 60$</tex> to solve for <tex>$r$</tex>, and then substitute it into the formula for the area of the circle to find the area.</reasoning>\n\n<answer>300</answer>\n<<unused54>/reasoning>\n<unused56>content/lessons/math/11-3-7_0.0_0_0.1.md\n<unused55><unused53>\nThis model is from [https://www.gutenberg.org/files/48561/48561-h/48561-h.htm#d1e254](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/48561/48561-h/48561-h.htm#d1e254).\nYou are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\n<reasoning>The formula for the area of a circle is <tex>$A = \\pi r^2$</tex>, where <tex>$r$</tex> is the radius of the circle. In this case, we are given the circumference of the circle, which is 60 cm. We can use the equation <tex>$C = 2\\pi r$</tex> to solve for <tex>$r$</tex>, and then substitute it into the formula for the area of the circle to find the area.</reasoning>\n\n<answer>300</answer>\n<reasoning>\n<answer>150</answer>\n<reasoning>\n<answer>75</answer>\n<reasoning>\n<answer>100</answer>\n<reasoning>\n<answer>60</answer>\n<reasoning>\n<answer>120</answer>\n<reas