  "You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nFind the vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that $\\mathbf{c} + 2 \\mathbf{a} = 5 \\mathbf{b}$, where $\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nFirst, we can calculate $\\mathbf{c} = \\mathbf{b} - 2 \\mathbf{a}$.\n\nSubstituting the given values, we have $\\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{c} + 2 \\mathbf{a} = 5 \\mathbf{b}$, we can set up the following equation:\n\n$\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = 5 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$\n\nSimplifying the equation, we have:\n\n$\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = 5 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$\n\nComparing the corresponding elements of the two matrices, we can see that the equation is satisfied. Therefore, $\\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n<unused56>problems/2021/15/202115-100.md\n<unused55>10 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} - 4 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{x} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}$ is a solution, we can conclude that $\\mathbf{x} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n<unused53>%% @title 15.2021.100\n%% @tags: 15.2021.100\n%% @author \n%% @date 2021-15-100\n%% @problem Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nYou are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nLet's start by solving the matrix equation $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}$. We can express $\\mathbf{x}$ as a linear combination of the columns of $\\mathbf{A}$, where the coefficients are the elements of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nTo find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, we need to solve the system of equations:\n\n```\nx1 + x2 - 2x3 = 3\nx2 + 2x3 = 2\n-2x1 + x2 + x3 = -3\n```\n\nSimplifying the system of equations, we have:\n\n```\nx1 + x2 - 2x3 = 3\nx2 + 2x3 = 2\nx1 - 2x2 - x3 = 3\n```\n\nWe can rewrite the system of equations as follows:\n\n```\nx1 + x2 + x3 = 3\nx2 + 2x3 = 2\nx1 - 2x2 + x3 = -3\n```\n\nAdding the two equations, we have:\n\n```\n2x1 + 3x2 + 2x3 = 0\n```\n\nSubtracting the third equation from the first, we have:\n\n```\nx1 + x2 = -1\n```\n\nSubstituting this value into the second equation, we have:\n\n```\nx2 + 2x3 = 2\nx2 + 2x3 = -1\n```\n\nSolving this system of equations, we have:\n\n```\nx2 + 2x3 = 2\nx2 + 2x3 = -1\n-3x3 = -3\nx3 = 1\nx2 + 2x3 = -1\nx2 = -3\n```\n\nNow that we have found the values of $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$, we can substitute them into the third equation to find $\\mathbf{x}_3$:\n\n```\nx1 + x2 + x3 = 3\nx1 + x2 + x3 = 3\n-3 + 2 - x3 = 3\n-x3 = 4\nx3 = -4\n```\n\nTherefore, the solution is $\\mathbf{x} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{x} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n<unused54>\n%% @solution Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\n%% @tags: 15.2021.100\n%% @author \n%% @date 2021-15-100\n%% @problem Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nFirst, we can write the augmented matrix:\n\n$\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & -2 & | & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & | & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & | & -3 \\end{pmatrix}$\n\nPerforming row operations, we can obtain the reduced row echelon form of $\\mathbf{A}$, which is:\n\n$\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & -2 & | & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & | & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & | & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$\n\nFrom this, we can see that $\\mathbf{A}$ has a pivot column, which means that the matrix has a solution. Therefore, $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}$ has a unique solution.\n\nNow, we can solve for $\\mathbf{x}$ by back substitution. From the reduced row echelon form, we can see that $\\mathbf{x}_1 = 3$, $\\mathbf{x}_2 = -1$, and $\\mathbf{x}_3 = 0$. Therefore, $\\mathbf{x} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -<unused56>problems/2021/15/202115-041.md\n<unused53>%% @title 15.2021.041\n%% @tags: 15.2021.041\n%% @author \n%% @date 2021-15-041\n%% @problem Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nYou are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nLet's start by solving the matrix equation $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}$. We can express $\\mathbf{x}$ as a linear combination of the columns of $\\mathbf{A}$, where the coefficients are the elements of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nTo find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, we need to solve the system of equations:\n\n```\n3x1 - 2x2 + 5x3 = -1\n5x1 + 7x2 - 5x3 = -2\n2x1 + 2x2 + x3 = 1\n```\n\nSimplifying the system of equations, we have:\n\n```\n3x1 - 2x2 + 5x3 = -1\n5x1 + 7x2 - 5x3 = -2\nx1 + x2 + x3 = 1\n```\n\nWe can rewrite the system of equations as follows:\n\n```\n3x1 - 2x2 + 5x3 = -1\n5x1 + 7x2 - 5x3 = -2\nx1 + x2 + x3 = 1\n```\n\nAdding the two equations, we have:\n\n```\n8x1 + 2x2 + x3 = 3\n```\n\nSubtracting the third equation from the first, we have:\n\n```\nx1 - x2 + 3x3 = 2\n```\n\nWe can rewrite the system of equations as follows:\n\n```\nx1 - x2 + 3x3 = 2\n8x1 + 2x2 + x3 = 3\n```\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second, we have:\n\n```\n-7x2 + x3 = -3\n```\n\nDividing the third equation by 3, we have:\n\n```\nx1 - x2 + 3x3 = 2\nx2 - x3 = -1\n```\n\nWe can rewrite the system of equations as follows:\n\n```\nx1 - x2 + 3x3 = 2\nx2 - x3 = -1\n```\n\nWe can rewrite the system of equations as follows:\n\n```\nx1 = 2 + x2 - 3x3\n```\n\nSubstituting this value into the second equation, we have:\n\n```\nx2 - x3 = -1\nx2 - 3x3 = -1\nx2 = -3x3 + 1\n```\n\nSubstituting this value into the first equation, we have:\n\n```\nx1 = 2 + x2 - 3x3\nx1 = 2 + (-3x3 + 1) - 3x3\nx1 = 5\n```\n\nTherefore, the solution is $\\mathbf{x} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{x} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n%% @solution Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\n%% @tags: 15.2021.041\n%% @author \n%% @date 2021-15-041\n%% @problem Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\mathbf{Ax} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pma