  'The following is a solution for a question which has been made into a problem. The problem is given below and a solution is given. You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nA cube-shaped block of butter with side length 6 inches is placed on a flat plate, melting into a puddle. As it melts, the butter spreads evenly, forming a rectangular shape that extends 2 inches beyond the plate on each side. If the area of the melted butter on the plate is 40 square inches, how thick is the melted butter?\n\n<reasoning>The area of the melted butter on the plate is 40 square inches. This is the sum of the areas of the original block of butter and the area of the melted butter on each side of the plate. The area of the original block of butter is 6 inches * 6 inches = 36 square inches. The area of the melted butter on each side of the plate is 2 inches * 6 inches = 12 square inches. Therefore, the area of the melted butter on the plate is 36 square inches + 12 square inches = 48 square inches. The thickness of the melted butter is the ratio of the area of the melted butter to the area of the plate, which is 48 square inches / 40 square inches = 1.2 inches. The melted butter is 1.2 inches thick.</reasoning>\n\n<answer>1.2 inches</answer>\n<unused54>= "6 inches * 6 inches = 36 square inches. The area of the melted butter on each side of the plate is 2 inches * 6 inches = 12 square inches. Therefore, the area of the melted butter on the plate is 36 square inches + 12 square inches = 48 square inches. The thickness of the melted butter is the ratio of the area of the melted butter to the area of the plate, which is 48 square inches / 40 square inches = 1.2 inches. The melted butter is 1.2 inches thick."\n</reasoning>\n\n<answer>1.2 inches</answer>\n<unused56><eos>'
