  '*The first step is to understand the problem. You should clearly understand the problem, what you need to do, and what information you have.\n*The second step is to think about your strategy. What approach will you take to solve the problem? What are the key ideas or concepts you can use?\n*The third step is to organize your thinking. What is the main idea or argument you are trying to make? What are the main points or details you will use?\n*The fourth step is to draft your argument. What will you write down to help you remember your thoughts and structure your writing?\n*The fifth step is to reflect on your argument. What do you think of your argument? Is it clear and well-supported? Are there any parts that need to be revised?\n*The sixth step is to revise your argument. Make any necessary changes to your argument based on your reflection.\n*The seventh step is to edit your argument. Make any necessary changes to your argument based on your reflection.\n*The eighth step is to finalize your argument. Submit your argument to the instructor.\n*The ninth step is to evaluate your argument. Ask yourself if your argument is persuasive, well-supported, and clear.\n*The tenth step is to present your argument. Share your argument with others.\n*The eleventh step is to revise your argument. Make any necessary changes to your argument based on feedback.\n*The twelfth step is to publish your argument. Publish your argument in a journal or on a website.\n*The thirteenth step is to celebrate your argument. Celebrate your argument and its success.\n*The fourteenth step is to reflect on your argument. Reflect on your argument and its success.\n*The fifteenth step is to repeat the process. Repeat the process for future arguments.\n*The sixteenth step is to share your argument. Share your argument with others.\n*The seventeenth step is to celebrate your argument. Celebrate your argument and its success.\n*The eighteenth step is to reflect on your argument. Reflect on your argume