  "You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nThe city council of a town has decided to raise taxes by 10%. The city council has estimated that this increase will result in a loss of 50% of the town's business and residential tax base. The city council has decided to reduce the tax rate by 30% to help offset the loss. What tax rate should the city council set to keep the total tax revenue constant?\n\nThis question is a good example of a problem that can be solved using a system of equations. The city council has estimated the amount of tax revenue that will be lost due to the increase in taxes. They have also estimated the amount of tax revenue that will be lost due to the reduction in taxes. The city council wants to find the tax rate that will keep the total tax revenue constant.\n\nThe system of equations can be written as:\n\n<tex>$\\begin{align*} 0.10 * (1 - 0.5) + 0.10 * (1 - 0.3) &= 0.10 \\\\ 0.30 * (1 - 0.5) + 0.30 * (1 - 0.3) &= 0.10 \\end{align*}$</tex>\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get:\n\n<tex>$\\begin{align*} 0.10 &= 0.10 \\\\ 0.10 &= 0.10 \\end{align*}$</tex>\n\nThis means that the tax rate should be set at 10% to keep the total tax revenue constant.\n\nIn this problem, we used the concept of a system of equations to find the tax rate that will keep the total tax revenue constant. The city council can use this information to make an informed decision about the tax rate.\n<unused56>html/09_08_2019_09_51_27_13027_268.html\n<unused53><unused55>\n<unused54>You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nIn a certain year, 1400 people were employed in a local manufacturing company. Each year, 5% of the employees were promoted to a higher position. If no one left the company, what was the number of employees at the end of the year?\n\nThis is a problem that can be solved using a pattern. The number of employees at the end of the year can be found by multiplying the initial number of employees by the factor 1.05 (5% increase each year).\n\nHere's the reasoning:\n\n- In the first year, there are 1400 employees.\n- In the second year, there are 1400 * 1.05 = 1470 employees (1.05 = 5%).\n- In the third year, there are 1470 * 1.05 = 1540 employees.\n- And so on...\n\nAt the end of the year, there are 1400 * 1.05^2 = 1400 * 1.1025 = 1555 employees.\n\nIn this problem, we used the concept of a pattern to find the number of employees at the end of the year. We used the factor 1.05 to increase the number of employees each year.\n<unused54>You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nA rectangular garden is 28 feet long and 14 feet wide. What is the area of the garden in square feet?\n\nThis is a problem that can be solved using a pattern. The area of the garden can be found by multiplying the length by the width.\n\nHere's the reasoning:\n\n- The length of the garden is 28 feet.\n- The width of the garden is 14 feet.\n- Area = length * width\n- Area = 28 * 14 = 392 square feet.\n\nIn this problem, we used the concept of a pattern to find the area of the garden. We used the length and width of the garden to calculate the area.<unused56>html/09_08_2019_09_51_27_13027_270.html\n<unused53><unused55><unused54>You are given a problem. Think about the problem and provide your reasoning. Place it between <reasoning> and </reasoning>. Then, provide the final answer (i.e., just one numerical value) between <answer> and </answer>.\n\nA man takes $1000 out of a bank for 12 months at an interest rate of 5% 